                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "This handles the password change function for IPMI users.  See related changes for password authentication here: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-net-ipmid/+/89728"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Vernon Mauery",
                        "email": "vernon.mauery@gmail.com",
                        "username": "vmauery"
                    },
                    "message": "I think I would prefer to just migrate to a better key size. I am rather fond of using https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd-id128.html to generate unique (per system) app IDs. So we would generate a 128-bit random ID that stands in for ipmi password. Then, we call systemd-id128 to have it hmac the system's ID with our ID and return the value. This gives us a nice value to use with things like our own hmac key.\n\nThe migration could just be a script that runs at boot if a given file is present (the current key file). Then it does the transformation, using the new systemd-id128 key and deletes the old file. We will need to make changes to all the repos that access the user password files (this repo, ipmid. I think netipmi uses the library from ipmid.) Then after a while, we remove the migration script."
                }
            ],
